from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('8');add=A.add
add('2.7-01','A moment measures a force’s turning effect about a pivot.','Its size increases with force and with the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the force’s line of action.','A spanner turns a nut because the hand’s force acts at a distance from the nut’s axis. Pushing near the end gives a larger turning effect than pushing near the nut with the same perpendicular force. The effect also depends on direction: pushing along the spanner toward the nut gives little turning effect. A crowbar turns about its contact point; scissors turn about their joint. Name the pivot before identifying a moment, and state clockwise or anticlockwise relative to the view used.','Explain why a longer spanner can loosen a nut with a smaller force.','The perpendicular distance to the force line is larger.~The same required moment can therefore be produced by a smaller force.')
add('2.7-02','Moment and work can both be expressed using force times a distance, but their distances describe different things.','Moment uses perpendicular distance to a pivot; work uses displacement along the force and is an energy transfer.','Moment has a rotational direction and is a vector quantity in the syllabus treatment; work is a scalar. Report a moment in N m and work in joules even though the units have equivalent dimensions. A person holding a stationary spanner may exert a moment without doing mechanical work on the stationary nut. If the nut rotates, a point of application moves and energy can be transferred. The existence of a lever arm does not itself establish a displacement.','Explain the difference between the distances used in moment and work.','Moment uses perpendicular distance from the pivot to the force line.~Work uses displacement in the direction of the force.')
add('2.7-03','The fulcrum is a lever’s pivot, the effort is the applied driving force and the load is the resistance being moved.','These names describe roles in the stated arrangement rather than fixed parts of every machine.','In a wheelbarrow the wheel axle acts as fulcrum, the contents provide much of the load, and the hands provide effort at the handles. A spanner has a turning axis at the nut and effort at the hand; the nut resists the rotation. On scissors the pivot is the joint, the fingers supply effort and the material resists cutting at the blades. Locate forces and their lines before measuring distances; the full length of a tool need not equal a particular moment arm.','State the fulcrum of a wheelbarrow when it is lifted by its handles.','The wheel axle is the fulcrum.')
add('2.7-04','The size of a moment is M = Fd, where d is the perpendicular moment arm.','With force in newtons and distance in metres, moment is in N m.','Convert centimetres to metres before substitution. A 15 N force with a perpendicular arm of 0.20 m produces 3.0 N m. If the force line passes through the pivot, its moment about that pivot is zero even when the force is large. For a sloping force, measure the shortest perpendicular distance from pivot to line of action rather than simply the length of the lever. Assign a clockwise or anticlockwise direction separately from the size.','Calculate the moment of a 12 N force with a perpendicular distance of 0.25 m from a pivot.','M = Fd = 12 × 0.25.~The moment has size 3.0 N m.')
add('2.7-05','Equilibrium requires both zero resultant force and zero resultant moment.','Balancing clockwise and anticlockwise moments alone does not establish translational equilibrium.','A stationary balanced beam has no tendency to accelerate in translation or rotation. Its support force must balance its downward loads, and its moments about a chosen pivot must balance. Two equal opposite forces whose lines do not coincide have zero resultant force but can produce a turning effect. Conversely, a force through the centre can accelerate an object without creating a moment about that centre. Test the two conditions independently; neither replaces the other.','State the two mechanical conditions for equilibrium.','The resultant force is zero and the resultant moment is zero.')
add('2.7-06','A balanced beam can test equality of clockwise and anticlockwise moments from vertical forces.','Perpendicular distances must be measured from the pivot to the vertical force lines.','First balance a uniform ruler on a pivot near its centre so that its own weight has negligible moment, or include that moment explicitly. Suspend known weights on opposite sides and adjust positions until the ruler rests horizontally. Measure each distance from the pivot, not from the ruler’s end. Calculate the moments in each direction and compare their sums. Repeat with different forces and positions. Keep the support stable, catch falling masses safely and do not use a hand to hold the beam in apparent balance while measuring.','Describe a beam experiment that tests the balance of moments.','Balance known vertical weights on opposite sides of a pivot and compare the sums of force times perpendicular distance for the two turning directions.')
add('2.7-07','A body in equilibrium has equal total clockwise and anticlockwise moments about a chosen pivot.','A suitable pivot eliminates the moment of a force acting through it.','For a light beam, a 6 N load 0.40 m to the left has an anticlockwise moment of 2.4 N m. A downward force 0.30 m to the right must therefore be 8 N for rotational equilibrium. The upward support force must also equal the total 14 N downward force. A heavy beam’s own weight must be included at its centre of mass when its force line does not pass through the pivot. Draw the force positions before substituting numbers.','Calculate the downward force 0.30 m right of a pivot needed to balance 6 N acting downward 0.40 m left of it on a light beam.','F × 0.30 = 6 × 0.40.~The balancing force is 8 N.')
add('2.8-01','The centre of mass of a suspended lamina lies vertically below its suspension point at rest.','Two suspension directions locate it at the intersection of the corresponding vertical lines.','Make a small hole near an edge of a thin irregular card and suspend it freely from a pin. Hang a plumb line from the same pin and mark its vertical line on the settled card. Suspend the card from another edge point and repeat. Their intersection locates the centre of mass; a third line is a useful check. The card must turn freely, not be clamped at an angle. For an irregular shape the intersection need not be at the visual centre and may even fall outside the material.','Describe how to locate a thin irregular card’s centre of mass using suspension.','Suspend it freely from two different points, mark each plumb-line vertical and locate the intersection.')
add('2.8-02','An object tends to topple when its weight’s line of action passes outside its support base.','A lower centre of mass or wider base generally permits a greater tilt before this occurs.','Consider a tall loaded trolley turning or standing on a slope. If its weight acts inside the base, a small tilt can produce a restoring moment; outside the edge, weight produces a toppling moment. Lowering a load reduces the horizontal shift of its centre of mass during a given tilt. Widening the base moves the supporting edge farther from the original weight line. Compare like arrangements and distinguish resistance to toppling from resistance to sliding: surface friction also influences what actually happens.','Explain why lowering a load can make a trolley more stable against toppling.','Its centre of mass is lower, so tilting moves the weight line sideways less.~A larger tilt is needed before the weight line passes outside the base.')
add('2.9-01','Friction opposes relative sliding, or the tendency to slide, between contacting surfaces.','Its magnitude depends on the contacting materials, surface condition and normal contact force.','A stationary shoe can experience friction that prevents slipping even without sliding. Friction on a walking person may act forward because it opposes the shoe’s backward tendency relative to the ground. Sliding friction often transfers mechanical energy into internal energy of the surfaces. Roughness can matter, but avoid treating visible roughness as a complete quantitative law for every pair of materials. Compare the same surfaces under controlled load when investigating their friction; distinguish the force needed to start motion from that during steady sliding.','Explain why friction can act on a shoe that is not sliding.','The shoe can have a tendency to slide relative to the ground.~Static friction opposes that tendency and prevents the slip.')
add('2.9-02','Lubrication and replacing sliding by rolling can reduce friction in suitable machines.','Friction provides useful grip and braking but can also cause wear and unwanted heating.','Oil can separate suitable moving surfaces, reducing direct interaction; ball bearings replace much sliding with rolling. These methods must fit the machine: reducing tyre-road friction would reduce useful grip. Brake pads rely on friction to reduce vehicle motion and transfer energy to the surroundings, so heating there accompanies the intended action. In bearings, the same heating wastes useful mechanical energy and can damage parts. State both the intended purpose and the surface pair when judging whether friction is beneficial.','Explain one useful and one unwanted effect of friction in a bicycle.','Tyre-road friction provides grip for control or braking.~Friction in moving bearings can cause wear and unwanted heating, so lubrication is useful there.')
A.misconception('MIS-05','A lever arm is not the displacement used to calculate work.','A force can have a moment about a pivot even when its point of application remains stationary.','A hand holds a horizontal bar at rest by pushing upward 0.50 m from its pivot. The hand force has a moment about the pivot, but the stationary point has no displacement, so no mechanical work is done on the bar by that force during the hold. Muscles can still consume chemical energy internally. Separating the body being studied from the person avoids treating effort felt by a person as proof of work on a stationary object.','A 10 N force holds a bar stationary 0.50 m from its pivot. Explain why its 5 N m moment does not mean it does 5 J of work on the bar.','The 0.50 m is a perpendicular moment arm, not a displacement.~The force’s point of application does not move, so its work on the bar is zero.')
C=A.choice
C('2.7-01','Which change increases the moment for the same perpendicular force?',('A larger pivot-to-force-line distance','Moment equals force times perpendicular arm.'),('A smaller arm','This reduces the product.'),('A force line through the pivot','This gives zero arm.'),('A change of force unit alone','Changing notation does not change the physical force.'))
C('2.7-02','Which quantity is a scalar?',('Work','It is an energy transfer.'),('Force','It has direction.'),('Moment','It has a rotational direction.'),('Velocity','It includes direction.'))
C('2.7-03','In scissors, which part serves as fulcrum?',('The joint','The blades rotate about this pivot.'),('The fingers','They provide effort.'),('The material','It supplies the cutting resistance.'),('The blade tip','It is away from the pivot.'))
C('2.7-05','Which pair of conditions establishes equilibrium?',('Zero resultant force and moment','Both translation and rotation are balanced.'),('Zero force alone','A couple can still turn the body.'),('Zero moment alone','Translation can still accelerate.'),('A large support force alone','Its balance with other forces is unspecified.'))
C('2.8-01','Where is the centre of mass of a freely suspended stationary lamina?',('On the vertical below the suspension point','Its weight line passes through the support.'),('At the lowest corner','The lowest point need not be the centre of mass.'),('At the suspension hole','The body generally hangs below that point.'),('At the largest visible edge','Edge length does not locate it.'))
C('2.8-02','Which change usually improves resistance to toppling?',('Widening the support base','A larger tilt is then needed to move weight beyond it.'),('Raising the centre of mass','This usually reduces the required toppling tilt.'),('Narrowing the base','This places an edge nearer the weight line.'),('Removing a supporting wheel','This may reduce the support region.'))
C('2.9-02','Which use needs substantial friction?',('A brake pad stopping a wheel','Friction transfers motion energy away.'),('A lubricated axle bearing','There friction is unwanted.'),('A freely turning pulley bearing','Friction there resists the intended movement.'),('A sliding machine guide intended to move easily','Lower friction is preferred there.'))
C('2.7-04','What moment does 8 N produce with a 0.15 m perpendicular arm?',('1.2 N m','Multiply 8 by 0.15.'),('53.3 N m','This divides by distance.'),('120 N m','This uses centimetres as metres.'),('0.01875 N m','This reverses the ratio.'))
C('2.7-07','A 4 N load has a 0.30 m arm. What load balances it on a 0.20 m opposing arm?',('6 N','The required moment is 1.2 N m.'),('4 N','Equal loads need equal arms.'),('2.67 N','This reverses the distance ratio.'),('12 N','This doubles the required balancing force.'))
C('2.7-04','A force line passes through a pivot. What is its moment about that pivot?',('Zero','The perpendicular arm is zero.'),('The force value in N m','An arm is still required.'),('The full beam length times force','Beam length is not the perpendicular arm here.'),('Its weight','Weight is a force, not a moment.'))
C('2.7-05','A light beam supports 3 N and 5 N downward and is stationary. What upward support is required?',('8 N','Vertical forces balance.'),('2 N','The downward loads add rather than subtract.'),('15 N','Multiplying the forces has no force-balance meaning.'),('Zero','That would leave a downward resultant.'))
C('2.7-02','A force holds a stationary lever. What is its work on the lever during the hold?',('Zero','The application point has no displacement.'),('Force times lever length','That length is not a movement.'),('Equal to its moment in joules','Moment and work are different quantities.'),('Force divided by time','That is not the work relationship.'))
P=A.part
P('2.7-03','State the meaning of fulcrum.','The pivot about which a lever turns.',1,'One mark for pivot.')
P('2.7-05','State the rotational condition for equilibrium.','The resultant moment is zero.',1,'One mark for zero net turning effect.')
P('2.7-02','Explain why a moment of 6 N m is not necessarily a transfer of 6 J.','Moment uses a lever arm; work requires displacement along force. The body may remain stationary despite the moment.',2,'One mark for distinct distance meanings; one for the absence of displacement preventing work.')
P('2.7-04','Calculate the moment of 9 N with a perpendicular arm of 0.40 m.','M = Fd = 3.6 N m.',2,'One mark for force times arm; one for 3.6 N m.')
P('2.7-07','Calculate the force at a 0.60 m arm that balances 12 N at a 0.20 m opposing arm.','F = (12 × 0.20)/0.60 = 4 N.',2,'One mark for equal opposing moments; one for 4 N.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot balancing effort vertically against reciprocal effort arm horizontally; the straight trend is expected for a fixed opposing moment.',unit='N m',meaning='The gradient of effort against reciprocal arm is the fixed opposing moment, because F = M/d. It is not the force or the arm alone.',method=['Balance a beam about a stable pivot and account for its own weight.','Fix a known load at a measured perpendicular arm on one side.','Choose an effort position on the other side and measure its arm from the pivot.','Adjust the effort force until the beam is horizontal and stationary, then record it.','Repeat for a range of effort arms, keeping the opposing moment fixed and securing suspended weights.'],control='Keep the load and its arm fixed, use vertical force lines, avoid pivot friction and prevent weights falling onto feet.',table='Record effort arm / m, reciprocal arm / m⁻¹ and balancing effort / N.',conclusion='A straight effort-versus-reciprocal-arm line supports Fd approximately constant; compare its gradient with the known opposing moment.')
